 ## Overview
-Hybrid Transparency is a rendering technique that mitigates popping artifacts in 3D Gaussian Splatting by combining depth-ordered alpha blending for the foremost $K$ fragments (the 'core') with order-independent accumulation for the remaining fragments (the 'tail') [S1, S2]. It solves the inconsistency of splat shapes and transparency across viewpoints caused by approximate sorting in standard splatting, avoiding the performance cost of hierarchical sorting or ray tracing [S2]. Unlike [Analytic Transmittance 3DGS](4440-analytic-transmittance-3dgs.md) which refines the density integration, and [SparseOIT Rasterizer](7562-sparseoit-rasterizer.md) which uses order-independent transparency for all fragments, Hybrid Transparency restricts expensive sorting to a small subset of fragments to maintain real-time performance [S1].
+Hybrid Transparency is a blending formulation that combines depth-ordered alpha blending for the first $K$ fragments (the 'core') with order-independent accumulation for the remaining fragments (the 'tail') to mitigate popping artifacts while maintaining real-time performance [S1, S2]. It solves the issue of incorrect per-pixel blending order caused by standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) approximations, which leads to visual popping during camera motion [S1, S2]. Unlike [Analytic Transmittance 3DGS](4440-analytic-transmittance-3dgs.md) which refines the density integration, or [SparseOIT Rasterizer](7562-sparseoit-rasterizer.md) which uses full order-independent transparency, Hybrid Transparency avoids global sorting and expensive order-independent techniques by only sorting the most visually significant front-most splats [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** A set of 3D Gaussians defined by mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}$, covariance $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ (or scales and orientation), opacity $o$, and color $\mathbf{c}$. Camera parameters.
-**Outputs:** Pixel color $C$ and accumulated alpha $\alpha$.
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Input:** A set of 3D Gaussians defined by mean $\boldsymbol{\mu}$, covariance $\boldsymbol{\Sigma}$ (or scales and orientation), opacity $o$, and color $\mathbf{c}$ [S1, S2].
+*   **Output:** Final pixel color $C$ and accumulated alpha $\alpha$.
 
